$ muster logs -h
Print or follow a process's log

Usage: muster logs [OPTIONS] [NAME]

Arguments:
  [NAME]  The process, as `muster ps` lists it; quote a name with spaces. Chosen from a list at a terminal when absent

Options:
  -f, --follow             Keep printing what the process writes, across restarts, until interrupted
  -p, --project <PROJECT>  The project to act on, by name or folder
      --screen             Print the process's current terminal screen instead of its log
  -n, --lines <N>          Print only the newest N lines, escapes removed (at most 2000)
      --since <CURSOR>     Print only output after this cursor, from an earlier `muster wait` or `muster logs --json`
      --grep <TEXT>        Print only lines containing this text
      --regex              Read --grep as a regular expression
      --errors             Print only error lines, each with its stack trace
      --json               Print the result as JSON; with --follow, one JSON object per log line
  -h, --help               Print help (see more with '--help')


$ muster attach -h
Show a process in this terminal and type into it

Usage: muster attach [OPTIONS] [NAME]

Arguments:
  [NAME]  The process, as `muster ps` lists it; quote a name with spaces. Chosen from a list at a terminal when absent

Options:
  -p, --project <PROJECT>  The project to act on, by name or folder
      --read-only          Watch without typing into the process or resizing it
      --json               Print a refusal or failure as JSON; an attach prints nothing else
  -h, --help               Print help (see more with '--help')


$ muster open -h
Show a process in a new terminal pane beside this one

Usage: muster open [OPTIONS] [NAME]

Arguments:
  [NAME]  The process, as `muster ps` lists it; quote a name with spaces. Chosen from a list at a terminal when absent

Options:
  -p, --project <PROJECT>      The project to act on, by name or folder
      --read-only              Watch without typing into the process or resizing it
      --direction <DIRECTION>  Which side of this pane the new one opens on; without it, right when the pane is wider than tall and each half keeps at least 40 columns, and down otherwise [possible values: right, down]
      --json                   Print the opened pane, or a refusal or failure, as JSON
  -h, --help                   Print help (see more with '--help')


